🚀 Week 5 – Day 7 Wrapper Classes & Autoboxing in Java ☕

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🧠 Introduction

Java provides Wrapper Classes to convert primitive data types into objects.

👉 Wrapper Classes are important because:

✔ Collections store objects, not primitives
✔ Utility methods become available
✔ Easier type conversions

Java also supports:

* Autoboxing
* Unboxing

These features automatically convert between primitives and wrapper objects.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 1. What are Wrapper Classes?

Wrapper Classes are object representations of primitive data types.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Primitive Types and Wrapper Classes

| Primitive Type | Wrapper Class |
| -------------- | ------------- |
| byte           | Byte          |
| short          | Short         |
| int            | Integer       |
| long           | Long          |
| float          | Float         |
| double         | Double        |
| char           | Character     |
| boolean        | Boolean       |

---

📌 Example

```java id="w1"
int number = 100;

Integer obj = Integer.valueOf(number);

System.out.println(obj);
```

---

🎯 Why Wrapper Classes?

✔ Needed in Collections Framework
✔ Utility methods available
✔ Supports object-oriented programming

---

📌 Example with ArrayList

```java id="w2"
import java.util.ArrayList;

ArrayList<Integer> list =
        new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
```

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔄 2. Autoboxing

Autoboxing is the automatic conversion of a primitive type into its corresponding wrapper object.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Example

```java id="a1"
int num = 50;

Integer obj = num;
```

---

🔍 Behind the Scenes

Java internally performs:

```java id="a2"
Integer obj =
        Integer.valueOf(num);
```

---

🎯 Benefits of Autoboxing

✔ Cleaner code
✔ Automatic conversion
✔ Useful with collections

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔄 3. Unboxing

Unboxing is the automatic conversion of a wrapper object into its primitive type.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Example

```java id="u1"
Integer obj = 100;

int num = obj;
```

---

🔍 Behind the Scenes

Java internally performs:

```java id="u2"
int num =
        obj.intValue();
```

---

💻 Complete Example

```java id="ex1"
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int age = 21;

        Integer ageObj = age;   // Autoboxing

        int value = ageObj;     // Unboxing

        System.out.println(ageObj);
        System.out.println(value);
    }
}
```

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🛠️ Useful Wrapper Class Methods

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔹 parseInt()

Converts String to int.

```java id="m1"
int num =
        Integer.parseInt("100");
```

---

🔹 valueOf()

Converts primitive/string to wrapper object.

```java id="m2"
Integer obj =
        Integer.valueOf("200");
```

---

🔹 toString()

Converts value to String.

```java id="m3"
String str =
        Integer.toString(100);
```

---

🔹 compareTo()

Compares wrapper objects.

```java id="m4"
Integer a = 10;
Integer b = 20;

System.out.println(
        a.compareTo(b));
```

---

📌 NumberFormatException Example

```java id="m5"
Integer.parseInt("abc");
```

---

❌ Output

```text id="m6"
NumberFormatException
```

---

📊 Autoboxing vs Unboxing

| Feature    | Autoboxing         | Unboxing           |
| ---------- | ------------------ | ------------------ |
| Conversion | Primitive → Object | Object → Primitive |
| Automatic  | ✔ Yes              | ✔ Yes              |
| Example    | int → Integer      | Integer → int      |

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Parsing invalid strings
❌ Comparing wrapper objects using `==`
❌ Ignoring null values during unboxing

---

📌 Example

```java id="m7"
